  Every visual decision is a typed property, `component.axis: value`, and
  settings.conf lists ALL of them, commented out at the current theme's
  values. Uncomment a line to pin it: it survives every theme change and
  every executable upgrade, and lightwebpres never rewrites your file
  (`series theme set` touches only the `theme:` line).
  Shared values themes provide, referenced by bare words:
    color.page    the page background
    color.ink     body text          color.ink-quiet   secondary text
    color.mark    what marks         color.call        what calls attention
    color.affirm  what affirms       (the "yes" verdict — posed as text)
    color.nav     the navigation hardware — progress dot, focus rings,
                  series-nav rule; furniture, never anything you write
    font.text / font.display / font.ui / font.mono
                  four stacks; each must end on a CSS generic family
  Component properties name what they paint — a few of the {property_count}:
    verdict.partial.fg: #8a4b00     one verdict, nothing else moves
    kicker.transform: none          kickers stop being uppercase
    cover.bg.to: #44475A            the cover becomes a gradient
    title1.shadow.fg: #33FF8866     a halo (a shadow with no offset)
    fact.strong.weight: normal      un-bold the fact-box emphasis
  A bare word is looked up in its type's namespace (`kicker.fg: ink-quiet`);
  a dotted word references another property (`title1.fg: cover.fg`).
  Errors name the file and the key — a mistyped property is a build error,
  never a silent no-op. A valid value that turns out to be the ground it
  will be painted on is not an error: `lightwebpres audit` names it, on the
  composed sheet, and the build still runs. CSS rules (new selectors,
  @font-face, media queries) belong in templates/custom.css, appended after
  the composed stylesheet so your rules win ties.

ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
  LWP_SOURCES_DIR    Articles directory (default: $LWP_SERIES_DIR/sources)
  LWP_TEMPLATES_DIR   Templates directory (default: $LWP_SERIES_DIR/templates)
  LWP_OUTPUT_DIR      Output directory (default: $LWP_SERIES_DIR/public)
  LWP_LANG            Language (default: fr)
  LWP_SERIES_DIR      Series directory (default: .) — used when no
                      [directory] argument is given
  LWP_LANGUAGE_DIR    Language directory (default: $LWP_SERIES_DIR/language)

SERIES DIRECTORY
  Written by `init`:
  series.json          List of articles + series metadata
  sources/            One .md file per article (+ *_article.md for the full
                        article); put images in sources/img/ and the build
                        copies them to public/img/
  templates/            settings.conf (your pinned values) and custom.css
                        (your rules) — those two are yours, and nothing but
                        you ever writes them
  language/             Created empty. The typography rules and the interface
                        strings live in this executable and are read from it;
                        a pack you put here overrides it for that language,
                        partially (missing keys fall back to the built-in
                        pack). `template write fr.json .` puts the built-in
                        one here to start from
  public/               Where the build writes (do not edit by hand)
  lightwebpres          Copy of the executable itself, for standalone use
  COPYING               GPLv3, and COPYING.EXCEPTION beside it — the copy
  COPYING.EXCEPTION     above travels with its licence

  Written by `build`:
  README.md             Generated from series.json (article list)
  .lwp-cache/nav.json   Read by build --only (spec §11.3.1) — derived state,
                        safe to delete, never edited by hand

  Not written by anything, and read if present:
  templates/nav.js      The navigation script. It lives in this executable
                        too; `template write nav.js .` installs a copy to
                        modify, and from then on the build uses the copy and
                        stops following the tool's fixes (spec §9.4.5)

ARTICLE FORMAT (.md)
  <!-- lwp:meta --> ... ---     Metadata block at the top of the file
  ---                           Separates slides, alone on its own line
  <!-- lwp:slide:TYPE -->       Marks a slide's type (absent = standard)
  key: value                    A slide field (one line = one value; no line
                                breaks inside a value for now)

SLIDE TYPES
{slide_types_block}

FIELDS RECOGNIZED IN A SLIDE
{slide_fields_block}

ARTICLE-LEVEL FIELDS (series.json entry, overriding the .md meta block)
  page_source (required, series.json only), page_dest, page_title, page_desc,
  card_title, card_desc, card_label, nav_title, nav_desc, author, license,
  date, status, comment — see GLOSSARY.md
  for every field's scope and
  fallback chain. author/license default from series_meta for the whole
  series; author/date/license are displayed (page footer + <meta> tags),
  page_desc feeds <meta name="description">. notes_placement, notes_tooltip
  and slide_page_numbers are set in series_meta or the article's meta
  block — never in an articles[] entry. So is slug_prefix, which puts a
  namespace in front of every card id on the page (§12.1.1).

  Only page_source is ever required: every other field falls back to the
  .md meta block, then to what the article already says (its cover's
  title and summary), then to a default. To read what that resolves to
  for a whole series — and which level each value came from — without
  building anything:
    lightwebpres status my-series
    lightwebpres status my-series --format json

  For ONE name, with the levels that lost as well as the one that won:
    lightwebpres resolve my-series page_title --article intro.md
    lightwebpres resolve my-series kicker.fg
    lightwebpres resolve my-series fact-label

NOTES (specifications.md §6.5)
  [^label] calls a note, [^label]: body defines it — standard Markdown, in
  a slide's free text or in the full-article file. The call links to the
  body, the body links back, and the label stays out of the page: what the
  reader sees is a position, so inserting a note renumbers nothing you
  wrote. A label is word characters only — letters, digits, underscore,
  accents and non-Latin scripts included; not `-`, not a space, not
  punctuation. Anything else is neither a note nor an error: the call
  ships as literal text, the body renders as an ordinary paragraph, and
  the label is in plain view. That is the one way a label reaches the
  page, and the sign it was not one.

  Two article-level <!-- lwp:meta --> fields, also settable in series_meta
  (the article wins):
    notes_placement: local   bodies at the foot of the unit that called
                             them — that card, or the end of the long-form
                             article. Numbering restarts in each card,
                             because a card is shareable on its own. Default.
    notes_placement: page    every body collected into one notes section at
                             the end of the page, numbered continuously
    notes_tooltip: on        also put the body's text on the call. Composes
                             with either placement, never the only carrier
                             (the body stays in the document). Default off.
  An unknown value is a build error naming the article.

  audit names them, none of them fatal: a label outside that pattern (the
  call and the body are reported separately, because they do not repair
  the same way), a call with no body, a body nothing calls, and a body
  written inside a raw HTML block (raw HTML is passed through verbatim,
  so it ships as literal text).

TYPOGRAPHY (non-breaking spaces) — this alters generated content, see specifications.md §7/§19
  The built-in French pack (language/fr.json) inserts a non-breaking space
  automatically in generated HTML: before ; : ! ? and %, after an opening «,
  between groups of 3 digits in a number the source already spaces out
  (e.g. "170 000"), between a number and million(s)/milliard(s)/dollar(s)/$,
  and after × or ≈ before a number. It only ever upgrades a space the
  source already has to a non-breaking one — it never inserts spacing or
  digit grouping that wasn't there, and a non-breaking space already in
  the source is always left exactly as written.

  Three article-level <!-- lwp:meta --> fields turn part or all of this
  off, per article, for its own page only:
    typo_units: off       disables the number/unit and ×|≈/number rules
    typo_thousands: off   disables the thousands-grouping rule
    typo: off             disables every rule for this article, not just
                          the two above (same effect as --no-typography,
                          scoped to one article)
  Any other value, or the field's absence, leaves the rule on (default).

  --no-typography (build/verify) disables the whole engine for that run —
  every rule, on every article, not just the fields above — for when you
  need the generated HTML to match the Markdown source's spacing exactly.

The framework is a single executable file, with no external dependency.
Python 3.8+ standard library only. Native UTF-8. On Windows
(no shebang), run: python lightwebpres <command>.
